## Technology Stack Guidelines

- Use Node.js (v18 or higher) as the JavaScript runtime for all backend services
- Build REST APIs using Express.js framework with proper middleware architecture
- Use TypeScript for type safety and better developer experience (recommended but not mandatory)
- Deploy backend services as Docker containers for consistent environments
- Store data in PostgreSQL database following relational database best practices
- Use Prisma as the ORM for type-safe database access and migrations
- Implement CI/CD pipelines with GitHub Actions for automated builds, tests, and deployments
- Structure the project with clear separation of concerns (controllers, services, middleware, utils)
- Use environment variables for configuration management
- Implement structured logging with Winston or Pino for debugging and monitoring

## Key Dependency Guidelines

- Use Express.js as the primary web framework for REST API development
- Implement JWT authentication using jsonwebtoken library
- Hash passwords with bcrypt before storing in the database
- Handle file uploads using Multer middleware
- Integrate with Amazon S3 using AWS SDK for image and document storage
- Validate request payloads using Joi or Zod schema validation libraries
- Document APIs using Swagger/OpenAPI with swagger-jsdoc and swagger-ui-express
- Write tests using Jest for unit tests and Supertest for API integration tests
- Use dotenv for environment variable management
- Implement CORS using the cors middleware
- Use Prisma Client for all database operations with type safety
- Never use dependencies with known security vulnerabilities or incompatible licenses

## Project Structure

The backend follows a layered architecture pattern with clear separation of concerns:

```
backend/
├── src/
│   ├── controllers/     # HTTP request handlers and route logic
│   ├── services/        # Business logic and orchestration
│   ├── middleware/      # Express middleware functions
│   ├── utils/           # Helper functions and utilities
│   ├── config/          # Configuration and environment setup
│   ├── types/           # TypeScript type definitions
│   └── constants/       # Application constants and enums
├── prisma/
│   ├── schema.prisma    # Database schema definition
│   ├── migrations/      # Database migration files
│   └── seed.ts          # Database seeding script
├── tests/               # Test files
├── uploads/             # Temporary upload directory
├── logs/                # Application logs
├── Dockerfile           # Docker container configuration
├── .env.example         # Environment variable template
└── package.json         # Dependencies and scripts
```

## Controller Layer Guidelines

### Purpose
Controllers handle HTTP requests, validate input, call appropriate services, and format responses.

### Best Practices
- Keep controllers thin - delegate business logic to services
- Validate request payloads using middleware or validation libraries
- Handle only HTTP-specific concerns (request/response, status codes, headers)
- Use async/await for asynchronous operations
- Call services for business logic and data operations
- Format responses consistently across all endpoints
- Handle errors by passing them to error handling middleware
- Use descriptive route names following REST conventions
- Group related routes using Express Router
- Document all endpoints with JSDoc comments for Swagger generation

### Controller Structure
```javascript
// controllers/order.controller.js
const orderService = require('../services/order.service');
const { validateOrderCreation } = require('../middleware/validation');

exports.createOrder = async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const userId = req.user.id; // From auth middleware
    const orderData = req.body;
    
    const order = await orderService.createOrder(userId, orderData);
    
    res.status(201).json({
      success: true,
      data: order,
      message: 'Order created successfully'
    });
  } catch (error) {
    next(error); // Pass to error handling middleware
  }
};
```

## Service Layer Guidelines

### Purpose
Services contain business logic, orchestrate operations, manage transactions, and interact with the database.

### Best Practices
- Implement all business logic in services, not in controllers
- Use Prisma Client for database operations
- Wrap multiple database operations in transactions for atomicity
- Validate business rules and constraints
- Handle errors with custom error classes
- Keep services focused on a single domain (orders, customers, delivery, etc.)
- Use dependency injection where applicable
- Implement proper error handling and logging
- Calculate derived values (e.g., delivery fees, totals) in services
- Emit events or notifications after state changes
- Document complex business logic with comments

### Service Structure
```javascript
// services/order.service.js
const { PrismaClient } = require('@prisma/client');
const prisma = new PrismaClient();
const { AppError } = require('../utils/errors');
const billingService = require('./billing.service');
const notificationService = require('./notification.service');

class OrderService {
  async createOrder(customerId, orderData) {
    // Validate business rules
    if (!orderData.items || orderData.items.length === 0) {
      throw new AppError('Order must contain at least one item', 400);
    }

    // Use transaction for atomicity
    const result = await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
      // Create order
      const order = await tx.orders.create({
        data: {
          customer_id: customerId,
          mart_id: orderData.martId,
          is_per_unit_piece: orderData.isPerUnitPiece,
          pickup_required: orderData.pickupRequired,
          // ... other fields
        },
      });

      // Create order items
      if (orderData.isPerUnitPiece) {
        await tx.order_items.createMany({
          data: orderData.items.map(item => ({
            order_id: order.order_id,
            item_id: item.itemId,
            item_counts: item.quantity,
            subtotal: item.subtotal,
          })),
        });
      } else {
        await tx.order_items_kg.createMany({
          data: orderData.items.map(item => ({
            order_id: order.order_id,
            service_id: item.serviceId,
            quantity: item.weightKg,
            unit_weight_price: item.pricePerKg,
            subtotal: item.subtotal,
          })),
        });
      }

      // Create bill
      await billingService.createBill(order.order_id, orderData, tx);

      return order;
    });

    // Send notification
    await notificationService.sendOrderConfirmation(customerId, result.order_id);

    return result;
  }

  async getOrderById(orderId) {
    const order = await prisma.orders.findUnique({
      where: { order_id: orderId },
      include: {
        order_items: {
          include: { clothes_items: true }
        },
        order_items_kg: {
          include: { services: true }
        },
        bills: true,
      },
    });

    if (!order) {
      throw new AppError('Order not found', 404);
    }

    return order;
  }

  // ... other methods
}

module.exports = new OrderService();
```

## Middleware Guidelines

### Authentication Middleware
```javascript
// middleware/auth.middleware.js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const { AppError } = require('../utils/errors');

exports.authenticateJWT = async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.replace('Bearer ', '');
    
    if (!token) {
      throw new AppError('No token provided', 401);
    }

    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = decoded;
    next();
  } catch (error) {
    next(new AppError('Invalid or expired token', 401));
  }
};

exports.authorize = (...roles) => {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (!roles.includes(req.user.role)) {
      return next(new AppError('Insufficient permissions', 403));
    }
    next();
  };
};
```

### Validation Middleware
```javascript
// middleware/validation.middleware.js
const Joi = require('joi');

exports.validate = (schema) => {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    const { error, value } = schema.validate(req.body, {
      abortEarly: false,
      stripUnknown: true,
    });

    if (error) {
      const errors = error.details.map(detail => ({
        field: detail.path.join('.'),
        message: detail.message,
      }));

      return res.status(400).json({
        success: false,
        errors,
        message: 'Validation failed',
      });
    }

    req.body = value;
    next();
  };
};
```

### Error Handling Middleware
```javascript
// middleware/error.middleware.js
const logger = require('../utils/logger');

exports.errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log error
  logger.error({
    message: err.message,
    stack: err.stack,
    url: req.originalUrl,
    method: req.method,
    correlationId: req.correlationId,
  });

  // Determine status code
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;
  
  // Format response
  res.status(statusCode).json({
    success: false,
    message: err.message || 'Internal server error',
    errorCode: err.code,
    ...(process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' && { stack: err.stack }),
  });
};
```

## Database Guidelines (Prisma)

### Schema Design
- Follow PostgreSQL best practices for table design and normalization
- Use UUIDs for primary keys for distributed systems
- Add appropriate indexes for frequently queried fields
- Use enums for fields with fixed values
- Leverage JSON fields for flexible schema parts (e.g., service_radius_km)
- Add timestamps (created_at, updated_at) to all tables
- Define foreign key constraints with appropriate cascade rules
- Use meaningful table and column names (snake_case for database, camelCase in Prisma models)

### Prisma Client Usage
```javascript
// Good: Use Prisma Client with proper error handling
try {
  const orders = await prisma.orders.findMany({
    where: {
      customer_id: customerId,
      order_status: 'delivered',
    },
    include: {
      bills: true,
      customers: {
        select: {
          full_name: true,
          email: true,
        },
      },
    },
    orderBy: {
      created_at: 'desc',
    },
    take: 10,
  });
} catch (error) {
  logger.error('Database query failed', error);
  throw new AppError('Failed to fetch orders', 500);
}

// Good: Use transactions for multiple operations
await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  const delivery = await tx.delivery.create({
    data: { /* ... */ },
  });
  
  await tx.orders.update({
    where: { order_id: orderId },
    data: { order_status: 'out_for_delivery' },
  });
});
```

## Error Handling Guidelines

### Custom Error Classes
```javascript
// utils/errors.js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode, code) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.code = code;
    this.isOperational = true;
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(message, errors) {
    super(message, 400, 'VALIDATION_ERROR');
    this.errors = errors;
  }
}

class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource) {
    super(`${resource} not found`, 404, 'NOT_FOUND');
  }
}

class UnauthorizedError extends AppError {
  constructor(message = 'Unauthorized') {
    super(message, 401, 'UNAUTHORIZED');
  }
}

module.exports = { AppError, ValidationError, NotFoundError, UnauthorizedError };
```

### Error Response Format
```json
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Validation failed",
  "errorCode": "VALIDATION_ERROR",
  "errors": [
    {
      "field": "email",
      "message": "Invalid email format"
    }
  ]
}
```

